  year = 2025,
  month = apr,
  journal = {Geophysical Research Letters},
  volume = {52},
  number = {7},
  pages = {e2024GL112516},
  issn = {0094-8276, 1944-8007},
  doi = {10.1029/2024GL112516},
  urldate = {2026-03-16},
  abstract = {Abstract             Glacier flow is typically modeled using a power-law rheology known as the Glen--Nye flow law, with the power  assumed to be 3. However, recent research and past observations suggest that  may better represent ice flow in some locations. We lack a quantitative understanding of how much  affects ice-loss projections, and its significance relative to other sources of uncertainty. Here, we test the effect of  versus  in a series of 300-year forward simulations of the Amundsen Sea Embayment, West Antarctica. We find that in periods of rapid grounding line retreat, uncertainty in  leads to a larger spread in ice-loss projections than the spread due to uncertainty in climate forcing. The spatial sensitivity of  is generally proportional to change in strain rates, so we expect regions undergoing more moderate dynamic change to be less sensitive to .           ,              Plain Language Summary             Our understanding of future sea level rise due to shrinking ice sheets is informed by mathematical representations of the movement of glaciers, in addition to melting and accumulation of ice. Fundamental to this mathematical theory is the Glen--Nye flow law, which describes solid ice as a viscous fluid that flows exponentially more easily under greater force. The numerical parameters of this equation are difficult to estimate, and most studies assume that the exponent ---which has the greatest impact on ice flow---is always equal to 3. Recent research suggests that a value of 4 better represents the actual behavior of glaciers in some places. We set out to answer whether this difference in  significantly affects mathematical simulations of glaciers enough that it should be considered in estimates of future sea level rise. We find that  does make a significant difference in the fastest retreating sector of the Antarctic Ice Sheet, because the ice is able to change much faster. Beyond the 21st century, the value of  in this region is at least as important to estimates of ice loss as the trajectory of future melt and snowfall patterns.           ,              Key Points                                                                Increasing Glen--Nye exponent from  to 4 leads to \% greater Amundsen Sea Embayment ice loss by 2100, \% by 2300 in our model                                                     Uncertainty in ice loss due to  is comparable to uncertainty from different high-emission (RCP8.5) climate forcings by 2100                                                     Regions most sensitive to  are located where changes in strain rates are largest, such as close to the grounding line and shear margins},
  langid = {english},
  file = {/Users/jonniebarnsley/Zotero/storage/L3ARVG9Y/Getraer and Morlighem - 2025 - Increasing the Glen–Nye Power‐Law Exponent Accelerates Ice‐Loss Projections for the Amundsen Sea Emb.pdf}
}

